  ],
  "text": "Neptune was found by doing arithmetic. They pointed a telescope where the maths said, and it was there. Uranus was drifting. Its position kept missing the prediction by a little, year after year. Something was pulling on it that nobody could see. Le Verrier in France and Adams in England worked out where that something had to be. Not just the direction. Its mass, its distance, and its position on a given night. An observatory in Berlin swung a telescope to the coordinates. They found it inside an hour, within one degree of the prediction. A planet discovered on paper before anybody had ever seen it. But the method has a failure case. Mercury drifted too, so Le Verrier predicted another planet. It does not exist. The answer was that gravity itself was slightly different from what he assumed. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
